<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Culture Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915 CE</a> – c. 1925 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Culture Movement was a Chinese intellectual movement of the 1910s and 1920s, launched by the writers of New Youth, that attacked Confucian tradition and promoted the vernacular, science and democracy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Intellectual life was transformed by the <b>New Culture Movement</b>, which promoted science, democracy and writing in the vernacular, championed by Hu Shi and by the writer Lu Xun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Behind the protests stood the aims of the <b>New Culture Movement</b> (1915–1921), whose leaders blamed Confucian values for the nation’s political weakness and called for “Mr. Science” and “Mr. Democracy” in place of “Mr. Confucius”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Critical readings have changed with the times: Marxist interpretation beginning in the <b>New Culture Movement</b> saw it as exposing feudal society's corruption, and since the 1980s critics have embraced its richness and aesthetics in a more multicultural context.</blockquote><a href="/work/dream-of-the-red-chamber">Dream of the Red Chamber · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
